q q BT 9 TL/F1 7.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 227.9553 25.51181 Tm (Script-Controlled ACL | )Tj[-4112.0007]TJ ( | Page 2)Tj T* ET Q q 1 0 0 1 57.02362 765.8661 cm 0 0 0 rg BT 19 TL/F2 15 Tf 1 0 0 1 0 4 Tm (ABSTRACT)Tj T* ET Q q 1 0 0 1 57.02362 687.8661 cm 0 0 0 rg BT 14 TL/F1 9.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 0 60.5 Tm (This project presents a script-controlled Access Control List \(ACL\) mechanism for restricting access to individual)Tj T* (records according to the value of a selected field. Traditional record-level access rules often depend on static role)Tj T* -.117791 Tw (or group permissions. Such rules can become difficult to maintain when access must change dynamically based on)Tj T* -.09271 Tw (record attributes. The proposed solution introduces a script-based authorization layer that evaluates a field value at)Tj T* 0 Tw (the time a record is requested and permits or denies access according to configurable conditions.)Tj T* ET Q q 1 0 0 1 57.02362 624.8661 cm 0 0 0 rg BT 14 TL/F1 9.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 0 46.5 Tm (The design separates authentication, record retrieval, policy evaluation, and authorization. A user may be)Tj T* (authenticated successfully but still be denied access to a particular record when the record does not satisfy the)Tj T* (applicable policy. This approach supports fine-grained authorization and can be adapted to employee, department,)Tj T* (project, status, ownership, or classification fields.)Tj T* ET Q q 1 0 0 1 57.02362 575.8661 cm 0 0 0 rg BT -.064589 Tw 14 TL/F1 9.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 0 32.5 Tm (The report follows the supplied project-report format and documents the problem, proposed solution, requirements,)Tj T* 0 Tw (architecture, planning, testing, results, advantages, limitations, conclusion, future scope, and an illustrative)Tj T* (source-code appendix.)Tj T* ET Q Q
